WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:00.781
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.781 --> 00:00:06.113
In this lesson we explore the Power Query Editor, where we clean and prepare

3
00:00:06.113 --> 00:00:07.636
data in Power BI.

4
00:00:07.636 --> 00:00:10.683
Here we have loaded the Financial Sample workbook.

5
00:00:10.683 --> 00:00:16.015
In Query Settings on the right, I rename the query to Financial Sample, so

6
00:00:16.015 --> 00:00:18.300
it is easy to find later.

7
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.667
When we close and apply, the queries appear in the Data pane of the

8
00:00:23.667 --> 00:00:25.000
report, ready to use.

9
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.483
Back in the Power Query Editor, the Queries pane on the left lists every

10
00:00:41.483 --> 00:00:42.658
table we loaded.

11
00:00:42.658 --> 00:00:45.400
Let's open the Financial Sample query again.

12
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.183
Under each column header there is a thin bar.

13
00:00:53.183 --> 00:00:58.134
Hover over it to see column quality: the share of values that are valid,

14
00:00:58.134 --> 00:01:00.610
that have errors, and that are empty.

15
00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:04.500
Here every value is valid, with no errors and nothing empty.

16
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:13.133
On the right, Applied Steps records each change in order: the source, the navigation,

17
00:01:13.133 --> 00:01:14.967
promoted headers, and changed type.

18
00:01:14.967 --> 00:01:20.100
You can click any step to see the data at that point, or remove

19
00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.300
a step you do not need.

20
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.107
Before we clean anything, it pays to understand the data.

21
00:01:44.107 --> 00:01:49.857
On the View tab, we can switch on three helpful options: column quality, column

22
00:01:49.857 --> 00:01:51.500
distribution, and column profile.

23
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.813
Column distribution adds a small chart under each header.

24
00:02:04.813 --> 00:02:09.474
It shows how many distinct and unique values each column has.

25
00:02:09.474 --> 00:02:12.863
Segment, for example, has only five distinct values.

26
00:02:12.863 --> 00:02:17.100
From here you can also remove duplicates in one click.

27
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:31.568
Column profile shows full statistics for the selected column at the bottom of the

28
00:02:31.568 --> 00:02:37.136
screen: the count, errors, empty values, distinct values, and a chart of how often

29
00:02:37.136 --> 00:02:38.330
each value appears.

30
00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:43.500
This is a quick way to spot problems before you build your report.

